<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hannibal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">247–c. 183 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Hannibal Barca · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hannibal Barca was a Carthaginian general who led the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. After crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> he won major victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, including at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, but could not force Rome to surrender. He was defeated at Zama, later took part in Carthaginian government, and remains the most famous figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="Hannibal" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fratelli Alinari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Philip V's treaty with <b>Hannibal</b> in 215 BCE drew Rome across the Adriatic in the First Macedonian War (214–205 BCE), and the Second and Third Macedonian Wars turned Rome into the arbiter of Greece.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The cities survived as Roman allies, supporting Scipio against <b>Hannibal</b> in 205 BCE; the Etruscans received Roman citizenship in 90 BCE, Sulla repressed Etruria in 82 BCE and Volterra capitulated in 79 BCE, and by 27 BCE the whole territory lay within the new Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo and Plutarch say <b>Hannibal</b> was received at the court of Artaxias I and, in a story they also tell, planned and supervised the building of Artaxata, though the tale is reckoned apocryphal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Zama in 202 BCE Masinissa's cavalry helped decide Rome's victory over <b>Hannibal</b>, and the peace of 201 BCE confirmed him as king of both Massylian and Masaesylian lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It funded <b>Hannibal</b>’s Italian campaign and long sustained the war against Rome despite Rome’s far larger citizen population and Italian alliance network.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Against Philip V of Macedon it fought at Chios and Lade in 201 BCE, and in the war against Antiochus III its fleet under Eudamus defeated a Seleucid squadron commanded by <b>Hannibal</b> off Side in 190 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in Gebze, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-206" aria-label="Carthage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 836 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hannibal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-206">What was here in 206 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A certain peace is better and safer than a victory in prospect; the former is at your own disposal, the latter depends upon the gods.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Scipio before Zama (Livy, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account of his words to Scipio &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Rarely does that man consider the uncertainty of events whom fortune hath never deceived.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Scipio before Zama (Livy, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account of his words to Scipio &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As far as relates to myself, time, (for I am returning to that country an old man which I left a boy,) and prosperity, and adversity, have so schooled me, that I am more inclined to follow reason than fortune.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Scipio before Zama (Livy, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account of his words to Scipio &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alexander, king of Macedonia; because, with a small band, he defeated armies whose numbers were beyond reckoning; and because he had overrun the remotest regions, the merely visiting of which was a thing above human aspiration.&#8221;</p><footer>Livy, History of Rome, book 35 &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; his answer at Ephesus when Scipio Africanus asked him who was the greatest general; Livy takes the story from the annalist Claudius, who had it from Acilius &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To Pyrrhus; for he first taught the method of encamping; and besides, no one ever showed more exquisite judgment, in choosing his ground, and disposing his posts…&#8221;</p><footer>Livy, History of Rome, book 35 &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; naming the second greatest general, in the same conversation with Scipio Africanus at Ephesus, as Livy reports it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
